## 2. Go Data Models  

```go
package inventory

import (
    "database/sql"
    "time"
)

// Site represents a physical location.
type Site struct {
    ID        int64          `db:"id"`
    Name      string         `db:"name"`
    Address   sql.NullString `db:"address"`
    City      sql.NullString `db:"city"`
    State     sql.NullString `db:"state"`
    ZipCode   sql.NullString `db:"zip_code"`
    Country   sql.NullString `db:"country"`
    CreatedAt time.Time      `db:"created_at"`
    UpdatedAt time.Time      `db:"updated_at"`
}

// Device represents a piece of hardware belonging to a Site.
type Device struct {
    ID           int64          `db:"id"`
    SiteID       int64          `db:"site_id"`
    Hostname     string         `db:"hostname"`
    AssetTag     sql.NullString `db:"asset_tag"`
    Vendor       sql.NullString `db:"vendor"`
    Model        sql.NullString `db:"model"`
    SerialNum    sql.NullString `db:"serial_num"`
    IPAddress    sql.NullString `db:"ip_address"` // stored as string for DB‑agnostic code
    MACAddress   sql.NullString `db:"mac_address"`
    Status       string         `db:"status"`
    PurchaseDate sql.NullTime   `db:"purchase_date"`
    WarrantyExp  sql.NullTime   `db:"warranty_exp"`
    CreatedAt    time.Time      `db:"created_at"`
    UpdatedAt    time.Time      `db:"updated_at"`
}
```

*Notes*

* `sql.NullString` / `sql.NullTime` are used for nullable columns – they avoid the “zero value = valid data” problem.  
* The `db` struct tags map the fields to column names for `sqlx`.  

